{
  "practice_data": {
    "method_card": {
      "steps": [
        "Find the extension: e = stretched length − natural length (convert to metres)",
        "For F = ke: identify two known quantities, substitute, rearrange for the third",
        "For Epe = ½ke²: square the extension first, then multiply by k, then by 0.5",
        "Check units throughout: F in N, k in N/m, e in m, Epe in J"
      ],
      "title": "Hooke's Law and Elastic Potential Energy",
      "content": "<p>Two equations to master for this topic.</p><p><strong>Hooke's law:</strong> \\(F = ke\\)</p><p>Force (N) = spring constant (N/m) × extension (m). Rearrange to find any of the three quantities. The key trap is units — extension must be in metres, not centimetres.</p><p><strong>Elastic potential energy (Higher):</strong> \\(E_{pe} = \\frac{1}{2}ke^2\\)</p><p>Energy stored in joules = half the spring constant multiplied by the extension squared. Because extension is squared, a small error in e has a large effect on the answer.</p><p><strong>Common pitfalls:</strong></p><ul><li>Extension = stretched length − natural length. Never substitute total length for extension.</li><li>Always convert cm to m before substituting (divide by 100)</li><li>The gradient of the straight section of a force–extension graph = the spring constant k</li><li>Beyond the limit of proportionality, F = ke no longer holds — the graph curves</li></ul>"
    },
    "topic_links": {
      "prerequisites": []
    },
    "exam_context": {
      "marks": "2-6 marks per calculation",
      "paper": "Physics (Higher Tier)",
      "frequency": "High — Hooke's law and Epe calculations appear most years in Separate Physics"
    },
